<h2>Insurance notes for western Nueces County</h2>
<p>All of Nueces County is in the TWIA designated catastrophe area, so some Robstown properties are insured for wind and hail by TWIA, while others have wind and hail on their regular homeowners, farm, or commercial policy. TWIA claims generally must be filed within one year of the date of loss, and properties built, altered, or repaired on or after January 1, 1988 need a windstorm certificate of compliance for TWIA eligibility. If your coverage is with a private insurer, Texas prompt-payment rules in Insurance Code Chapter 542 set timelines for acknowledging and deciding claims. Our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> can help you track dates.</p>
<p>Flash flooding is the other major risk here. Flood coverage comes from the NFIP or a private flood policy, usually after a 30-day waiting period. According to FEMA data (April 2025), unincorporated Nueces County participates in the NFIP but not the Community Rating System; check FEMA's Community Status Book for the City of Robstown's status. Permit rules inside Robstown city limits are set by the city, so check with city offices before repairs.</p>

<h2>Claim issues on farms and rural properties</h2>
<p>Nueces County's NCEI records include 287 thunderstorm wind reports, 110 of them in May, and 256 flash flood reports. In the Robstown area, that translates into a few recurring claim issues:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Metal buildings and barns.</strong> Wind can buckle panels, pull fasteners, and rack frames. Damage to purlins and connections is easy to miss from outside.</li>
<li><strong>Separate structures and limits.</strong> Detached garages, shops, and barns may fall under an "other structures" limit or be scheduled individually on a farm policy.</li>
<li><strong>Crops and equipment.</strong> Crop losses are usually handled under separate crop insurance, not a property policy, while farm equipment may be covered under its own schedule.</li>
<li><strong>Manufactured homes.</strong> The May 8, 2025 storm showed how vulnerable mobile homes and carports are to extreme wind.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How we help</h2>
<p>We walk the full property, including outbuildings, fences, carports, and equipment structures where they are insured, and we document roof, siding, window, and interior damage with photos and measurements. We then prepare an itemized estimate and handle communication with your insurer. If your claim was denied or underpaid, see our pages on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied claims</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claims</a>.</p>
